message.content:
I see a potential double-free here! In the `X_STRIPE` path:

```c
if (!out) {
    if (ulen >= INT_MAX)
        return NULL;
    if (!(out_free = out = malloc(ulen))) {
        return NULL;
    }
    *out_size = ulen;
}
if (ulen != *out_size) {
    free(out_free);
    return NULL;
}
```

If `out` is NULL, `out_free = out = malloc(ulen)`. Then if `ulen != *out_size`, `free(out_free)` is called. But `out_free` is only set when `out` was NULL. If `out` was non-NULL, `out_free` is NULL, so `free(NULL)` is a no-op. That's fine.

But wait, in the recursive call:
```c
if (!rans_uncompress_to_4x16(in+c_meta_len, in_size-c_meta_len, outN + idxN[i], &olen)
    || olen != ulenN[i]) {
    free(out_free);
    free(outN);
    return NULL;
}
```

Here `outN` is freed. But `outN` was allocated once and used for all N streams. If the recursive call fails, `outN` is freed and NULL is returned. But the caller might also free `outN`... no, the caller doesn't have access to `outN`.

